# >>> AI WORKFLOW MANAGED >>>
# Installed by ai-dev-workflow-template install.sh — keeps the workflow
# footprint out of this project's git history.
.ai/
.claude/
.agents/
# <<< AI WORKFLOW MANAGED <<<

# Two files under .claude/ are NucleOS, not the workflow footprint the block above excludes.
# core/src/autopilot.rs reads settings.json and refuses to activate autopilot unless it registers
# ask_daemon.py and that script is present on disk — the PreToolUse hook is the single point where
# the safety gate meets a running tool. Excluding them made them tracked-but-uncommittable:
# editable forever, and a fix to the security hook could never land.
#
# Deliberately below the managed block so the template's installer can rewrite its own lines
# without taking these with it. The directory has to be re-included before a file inside it can be:
# git never descends into an excluded directory, so `!.claude/settings.json` alone does nothing.
!.claude/
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json
!.claude/hooks/
.claude/hooks/*
!.claude/hooks/ask_daemon.py

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rust + Tauri + Node build artifacts
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/target/
# A second target directory, for building tests while the daemon is running out of the first one:
# `cargo test` relinks the binary, and Windows refuses to replace an executable that is in use.
/target-test/
# And the same directory under the crate, which is where `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` lands when tests are
# built from `core/` rather than from the root.
/core/target-test/
/shell/node_modules/
/shell/dist/
/shell/src-tauri/target/
# Output of `node scripts/preview-shots.mjs` — pictures to look at, not a baseline to diff.
/preview-shots/
*.db
*.db-journal
sidecars/**/*.exe

# TypeScript incremental-build artifacts (emitted by `tsc -b`)
*.tsbuildinfo
/shell/vite.config.js
/shell/vite.config.d.ts

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Agent instruction files — local only, never committed
# (patterns without a leading slash match at every depth)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md

# `*.md` used to sit here and it took the project's own documentation with it. Already-tracked
# files stayed tracked, so nothing was lost — but no new README could ever be added, which is the
# `ask_daemon.py` trap again: editable forever, uncommittable forever. A repository about to gain
# a remote needs to be able to have a README.
#
# What it was reaching for is named directly instead. These are the files the hard constraint
# names, at any depth; `.githooks/pre-commit` enforces the other half of the same rule, that a
# tracked one may only ever carry an empty template.
MEMORY.md
DECISIONS.md
memory.md
decisions.md
memory-archive.md

# Brainstorming companion scratch (HTML mockups + event log). Agent working
# material — local only. The `*.md` rule above already covers the specs it emits,
# but the mockups are .html and would otherwise be committable.
.superpowers/

# Local git hooks (core.hooksPath) — local only, never committed.
# A rule rather than a habit: without this the directory is merely untracked, one `git add -A`
# away from entering history. Recreate it with scripts/install-hooks.sh.
.githooks/
.vscode/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Design and docs — local working material
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These were purged from the history rather than merely untracked, and the difference is the
# whole point: the prototypes and the integration notes were already committed, and a
# repository that gains a remote publishes its history, not its checkout. Untracking them
# would have left them one `git log` away from anyone who cloned it.
#
# The files themselves stay on disk. `docs/` keeps exactly one tracked file so the directory
# survives a fresh clone.
design/
docs/*
!docs/.gitkeep
shell/target-test/
